<p>"Female body measurements average." The first result tells me the average American woman is 5'4", bust 38", waist 34", hips 40". So I'm taller than average, slimmer everywhere else. The ratio of my waist to my hips comes out around .69, which apparently gets described as "hourglass" in fashion articles. Great. I'm an hourglass. Marcus was a rectangle and now I'm an hourglass. What a promotion.</p>

<p>"How to measure bra size." This is where things get insane. I click through three different guides and get three different answers. My 27-inch underbust and 34-inch bust could make me a 28DD, a 30D, or a 32C depending on which brand's chart I trust. The sports bra Marlene gave me was a B and it felt fine, so I'm going to trust the woman who spent ten seconds assessing me over three conflicting websites. Probably a 32B for now. Bra sizing isn't a science. Bra sizing is astrology for clothing.</p>

<p>"Female anatomy diagram." I click a medical site with clinical cross-section illustrations, labels everywhere. Mons pubis. Labia majora. Labia minora. Clitoris. Clitoral hood. Urethral opening. Vaginal opening. Cervix. Uterus. Ovaries. Fallopian tubes.</p>

<p>I have all of this inside me. Not theoretically. Right now, sitting cross-legged on this mattress with the laptop warming my thighs, there are ovaries inside my pelvis producing estrogen that's making my skin soft and giving me mango cravings. There's a uterus in there that will, at some point, decide to shed its lining, and I will bleed from between my legs for several days while my abdomen cramps.</p>

<p>I read about the menstrual cycle for twenty minutes. Follicular phase, ovulation, luteal phase, menstruation. A 28-day cycle, give or take. Cramps, bloating, mood swings, breast tenderness, cravings, fatigue, headaches. The list of symptoms reads like a chronic illness fact sheet.</p>

<p>"This will happen to me," I say to the laptop screen. "Monthly. For decades."</p>

<p>I close the tab. Open it again. Read the section on managing cramps. Close it again.</p>

<p>"How to put on a bra." A YouTube tutorial by a woman in a bright pink bra standing in front of a ring light. She demonstrates the lean-forward-and-scoop technique, hook the bra behind your back, lean forward so your breasts fall into the cups, then scoop the tissue from under your armpits into the cups with your hands. I pick up the sports bra off the floor and try it. Hook, lean, scoop. The fit is immediately better, my breasts sitting higher, more centered, actually cupped instead of just compressed.</p>

<p>"Oh." I stand up and look in the mirror. The sports bra looks like it belongs on my body now instead of just being strapped to it. One video changed the fit completely. How do women learn all of this? Is there a class? Is it osmosis? Do their mothers teach them at thirteen while they're crying in a department store fitting room?</p>

<p>Nobody taught me. Nobody's going to teach me. I'm learning everything from scratch, from search results and tutorials and the kind of trial and error Marcus never had to think about because Marcus's body didn't come with an instruction manual that got lost in transit.</p>

<p>I keep going. "Skincare routine for women." Cleansers, toners, moisturizers, serums, SPF, exfoliants, retinol. Marcus's skincare routine was water. Sometimes soap. Apparently that was a war crime against my pores.</p>

<p>"How to brush tangled hair." Start from the bottom, work in sections, don't just yank from the roots like a caveman. I don't own a brush, but I need to buy one soon.</p>

<p>The research spiral goes on for two hours, tab after tab. I fall into a hole of women's health forums and come out the other side knowing more about vaginal pH than I ever wanted to know. I learn about discharge, apparently normal and constant, and yes, I've noticed it, a slight dampness in my panties I thought was sweat but turns out to be my body just doing its thing all day every day.</p>

<p>I learn about UTIs and why women get them more often than men. I learn about yeast infections and bacterial vaginosis and the importance of cotton underwear and not sitting around in wet bathing suits. I learn that I should never, ever douche. I learn the vagina is self-cleaning. This fact is simultaneously reassuring and deeply strange.</p>

<p>My brain is full. I close the laptop and sit on the bed in my sports bra and panties, staring at the wall.</p>

<p>I'm female and I know almost nothing about what that means, and the gap between what I need to know and what I actually know is so vast it makes me dizzy. But I'm filling it in. One search at a time, one fact at a time. This body is mine whether I chose it or not. I need to understand it.</p>

</div>

